class.description:
[ResourceTextSerializer] maps the properties of a [Resource] that have [constant @GlobalScope.PROPERTY_USAGE_STORAGE] to text that players and other tools can read and edit. The same mapping is used by [ResourceSaver] and [ResourceLoader] for the file extensions [code].json[/code], [code].ini[/code] and [code].csv[/code], so in most cases a call to [method ResourceSaver.save] and [method ResourceLoader.load] is all that is needed. The methods of this class do the same with strings, for example to send a resource over the network or to store it in a database. [codeblocks] [gdscript] var settings = AudioBusSettings.new() # A Resource with class_name AudioBusSettings. settings.master_volume = 0.8 ResourceSaver.save(settings, "user://audio.json") ResourceSaver.save(settings, "user://audio.ini")
var loaded = ResourceLoader.load("user://audio.json") as AudioBusSettings print(loaded.master_volume) # Prints 0.8
# The same as text, without a file: var text = ResourceTextSerializer.resource_to_json(settings) var copy = ResourceTextSerializer.json_to_resource(text) [/gdscript] [csharp] var settings = new AudioBusSettings(); // A [GlobalClass] Resource. settings.MasterVolume = 0.8f; ResourceSaver.Save(settings, "user://audio.json"); ResourceSaver.Save(settings, "user://audio.ini");
var loaded = ResourceLoader.Load<AudioBusSettings>("user://audio.json"); GD.Print(loaded.MasterVolume); // Prints 0.8
// The same as text, without a file: string text = ResourceTextSerializer.ResourceToJson(settings); var copy = (AudioBusSettings)ResourceTextSerializer.JsonToResource(text); [/csharp] [/codeblocks] [b]JSON:[/b] one object per resource. The root object has the keys [code]"$type"[/code] (the class name, for example [code]"AudioBusSettings"[/code]) and [code]"$format_version"[/code] (see [constant FORMAT_VERSION]), followed by one key per property in property order. Nested resources are nested objects, arrays of resources are arrays of objects. A nested object only has a [code]"$type"[/code] key if its class differs from the declared type of the property. A [code].json[/code] file without a [code]"$type"[/code] key that names a [Resource] class is still loaded as [JSON], as before; [method ResourceLoader.load] with a type hint (for example [code]load(path, "AudioBusSettings")[/code]) also reads such files as that type. [b]INI:[/b] one section per resource. The root resource is in [code][root][/code] with the key [code]$type[/code]; a nested resource is in its own section named after its path, for example [code][root/entries/0][/code]. By default the values use the Variant syntax of [ConfigFile] ([code]name="Ann"[/code], [code]size=Vector2(1, 2)[/code]). With [member ProjectSettings.filesystem/resources/text_formats/ini_plain_values] or the option [code]"plain"[/code], values are written without type syntax ([code]name=Ann[/code], [code]size=1, 2[/code], [code]color=#ff0000ff[/code]), so other tools can read them. Both styles are read. [b]CSV:[/b] only for list resources: a resource with exactly one typed array of resources (for example [code]Array[Entry][/code]) whose elements have only simple properties (no resources, arrays or dictionaries). The file has a header row with the property names and one row per element. A CSV file contains no type, so it must be loaded with a type hint: [code]ResourceLoader.load("user://voices.csv", "VoiceList")[/code]. A resource that does not fit gives an error and no file is written; check it first with [method can_save_as_csv]. [b]Values:[/b] [Color] is written as [code]"#rrggbbaa"[/code] when that is exact, otherwise as an array of floats; vectors, rectangles and matrices as arrays of numbers; enums as their names (see [member ProjectSettings.filesystem/resources/text_formats/enums_as_names]); [StringName] and [NodePath] as strings. A resource that is saved in its own file is written as its path and loaded from that path. When loading, a key that is missing keeps the default value of the class, and an unknown key is skipped with a warning. Values are converted to the type of the property, so a hand-edited [code]"0.5"[/code] or [code]"yes"[/code] is accepted for a [float] or [bool] property. [b]Note:[/b] JSON has only one number type. Integers above 2^53 lose precision, and properties of type [Variant] get [float] values back where [int] values were saved. In plain INI and in CSV, properties of type [Variant] come back as [String]. [b]Note:[/b] [code].csv[/code] files in [code]res://[/code] are imported as translations by the editor. Keep CSV resources in [code]user://[/code], or set their importer to "Keep File" in the Import dock. [b]Note:[/b] The methods of this class can be called from any thread.
